WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.100
Since the beginning of mankind as a species,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.060
weapons have been some of our cleverest and most important inventions.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.860
Over hundreds and thousands of years of war and killing,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.560
humanity has continued to devise new ways to kill one another.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.120
Often in brutal fashion.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.160
From the club, to the spear

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.980
to the bow, the catapult, the musket

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.480
up till today, with those nukes we looked at in the last video.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.080
They all serve the same purpose

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.820
but, over the course of several international conventions

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.600
some of these weapons have been banned from warfare for being too inhumane

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.340
If the sole purpose of these devices is to kill the enemy,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.380
What makes one method more inhumane than another?

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.960
Lets say you and I are on different sides of a military conflict in the mid 1600s

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.000
We're taking shots at each other with our horribly inaccurate rifles, until finally you get a hit.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.580
Luckily for me your bullet just grazed me.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.560
Somewhat less luckily I die from infections a few days later

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:51.440
Why?

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.820
Because it the 1600s and you like to store your bullets in corpses or latrine pits

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.860
to make sure that if you do manage to hit somebody, they die from infection.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.060
This storing of bullets in poisonous or infectious substances was the catalyst for the Strasbourg Agreement of 1675

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.340
The first ever international agreement banning the use of chemical weapons

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:15.160
The general sentiment was that using such an indirect and unpleasant means to kill the enemy was not befitting the principles of "civilized warfare".

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.660
Lets fast forward to the 22nd of April, 1915

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.080
The second battle of Ypres in Belgium during WW1 saw the first successful mass use of poison gas in war.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.660
The german army released 171 tons of chlorine gas over a stretch of about 6.5 kilometers.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.320
Chlorine gas is a really nasty substance.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.000
It irritates the eyes and skin

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.340
and when inhaled it reacts violently with the human body causing the lungs to fill with fluid.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.480
Those unlucky enough to be caught in the gas cloud

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.580
often drown painfully from the fluid inside their own lungs

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.380
As chlorine gas was a rather new development in weaponry

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.060
the methods the germans used to deploy it was quite crude.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.560
The chlorine was stored in nearly 6000 cylinders

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.460
weighing 41 kilograms each.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.180
Which the germans carried by hand to the frontlines.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.580
The canisters was opened by hand as soon as the winds shifted towards the French

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.800
Releasing a sweeping clump of noxious gas.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.980
The germans themselves suffered many injuries and deaths from their own gas

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.600
as it was impossible to control once out of the canisters.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.480
When the chlorine reached the French lines

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.260
within 10 minutes 6000 had died due to aspyixiation

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.100
and tissue damage to the lungs.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.100
Since chlorine gas is denser than air

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.100
it quickly flown down into the trenches

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.620
causing the French troops to scramble over the top to escape...

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.620
... right into german gunfire.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.060
Chlorine gas and several other poisonous chemicals

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.240
continued to see use throughout the rest of the war.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.340
Accounted for 1.3 million casaulties

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.240
and were the driving factor behind the well-known Geneva Protocol of 1925

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.920
It's full title was:

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.500
The Protocol for the Prohibition of the Use in War of

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.440
Asphyxiating, Poisonous or Other Gases

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.660
and of Bacteriological Methods of Warfare.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.640
Doesn't exaltly roll off the tongue...

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.780
In essence, the Geneva Protocol is a treaty

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.380
that bans the use of chemical and biological weapons in international armed conflicts.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:49.880
 

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.360
Like the Strassburg Aggreement.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.020
The thinking behind the generic protocol was that chemical weapons

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.880
caused unnecessary pain and suffering in its victims

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.560
as well as significant mental and emotional trauma

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.940
several years later WWII came along

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.840
and with it a new batch of devious weapons

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.060
the Japanese employed horrible

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.830
booby traps to defend their homeland

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.570
the most famous being the hidden spike fits

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.460
soldiers would step on a thin layer of

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.140
foliage and tumble down into rows of

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.420
sharpened bamboo spikes to make matters

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.610
worse the Japanese often coded despite

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.440
his human or animal feces which led to

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:19.760
terrible infections among the injured

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.490
these traps killed and maimed thousands

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.530
of troops over the course of the war the

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.150
Americans had their own new toys as well

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.980
namely the two atomic bombs that

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.230
decimated Hiroshima and Nagasaki world

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.120
WWII also saw the devastating use of napalm

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.580
napalm was the ideal incendiary

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.350
weapons because it was incredibly simple

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.600
and cheap to produce it was stable and

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.280
it stuck to whatever it hit all those

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.380
features made napalm very popular and

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:43.640
accounted for more dead Japanese

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.260
soldiers and civilians and the two

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.489
atomic bombs

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:48.140
once the dust of the Second World War

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.060
settled America enjoyed relative peace

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.130
until the disastrous vietnam war with

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:53.660
the jungle terrain came new means of

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.820
killing what better way to clear foliage

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.050
and flush out the enemy then by using

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.900
fire a flamethrower used extensively in

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.310
both world wars proved itself

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.140
particularly useful and torching entire

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.840
villages and hideouts napalm bombs also

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.280
saw extensive use with the u.s.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.130
reportedly dropping about 388,000 tons

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.180
of it over the course of the war

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.020
compared to 16,500 times in World

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.239
to the Vietnam War was hugely unpopular

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:18.100
among the American public for many reasons

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.050
among those were concerned that

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.540
these fire weapons were cruel and inhumane

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.000
sure enough on october tense 1980

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.560
the united nations convention on

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.660
certian conventional weapons came to a close and

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.100
with it came to legislation that

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.270
prohibited the use of many different

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.730
types of weapons incendiary devices are

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:34.870
listed under what's referred to as

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.180
protocol 3, which restricts their use in

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.780
any situation in which civilians are present

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.720
interestingly napalm isn't entirely banned it

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.400
can be used to set fire to forested

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:45.880
areas that conceal enemy troops or

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.770
vehicles but it's strictly prohibited

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.870
in civilian inhabited areas

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.450
other weapons ban by the CCCW include any form

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.700
of gas, including tear gas, which can still

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.230
be used in domestic riots, oddly enough,

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.170
Spike Pits, undetectable fragment weapons

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:00.880
meaning any weapon with the primary

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.410
effect of leaving shrapnel in human body

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.510
that is undetectable by x-ray

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.310
also included are non self-destructing

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.410
and non self-deactivating mines outside

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.870
fenced monitored and marked areas

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.550
these mines must also be removed from the

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.590
conflict is resolved and finally perhaps

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.390
more relevant to today's battlefield

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.580
blinding laser weapons, any weapon with

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.050
the ability to cause permanent blindness

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.000
or severe irreversible eye damage is

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:22.870
strictly prohibited.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.900
so what do we make of all this, even

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.060
though we have a long list of prohibited weapons

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.950
what's the common thread? what exactly

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:31.780
makes them more inhumane than a bayonet

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.210
or a 50 caliber machine gun? As you may

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.920
have guessed the main concern is not for

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.360
the combatant but for civilian

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.850
populations the protocols are very firm

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:40.990
on the position that indiscriminate

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.150
weapons (weapons that cannot be used to

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.592
target only hostile troops) must never be used

00:05:45.592 --> 00:05:47.290
that includes cluster bombs they

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:49.300
can blanket a wide area large explosives

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.010
like nuclear bombs and hidden weapons

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.170
such as mines and booby traps, of course

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.510
the secondary concern for the CCW was

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:56.740
indeed the humanity of the combatants

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:58.600
death at the hands of another is

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.340
certainly a tragic fate but at least

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.630
nations have been able to come together

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.340
and recognize that no one deserves to

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.890
suffer unnecessarily the future of war

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.690
is bound to be interesting with later

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.430
restrictions and technological advances

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.169
maybe we'll even see a complete ban on

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:13.690
human involvement in war computers and

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.280
drones of all sorts stand poised to

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.169
change the battlefield forever we'll

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:18.520
just have to wait and see how long it

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.010
takes that future to arrive as always

